00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.780
So you mentioned that
Travis CI-- the CI stands

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:25.380
for Continuous Integration.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:26.796
Why don't you tell
us a little bit

00:05:26.796 --> 00:05:29.430
about what is Continuous
Integration for people

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:30.670
that don't know what it is?

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:31.420
MATHIAS MEYER: OK.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:35.490
So a lot of this goes back
to extreme programming,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:39.530
where two of the practices
were you build every commit.

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:42.730
Like, everything you check
in is going to be tested.

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:47.580
And the other
practice is-- I forgot

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.935
what the other practice was.

00:05:49.935 --> 00:05:51.560
Anyway, I'm going to
focus on that one,

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.340
because that's the best one.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.000
So Continues Integration
focuses around the idea

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.470
that every change you
make to the codebase

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:01.170
is run in an
isolated environment.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:04.710
You test those changes and
you build, compile something,

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.630
and then you run a test
suite that hopefully exists

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:10.160
for this project to make
sure that the change that you

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:15.014
made still works in the bigger
picture of the test suite.

00:06:15.014 --> 00:06:15.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.410
So I'm assuming that with
that, basically, what you're

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:21.400
trying to do is to try to get
a build that always builds,

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.615
and as soon as someone
messes it up, you just know.

00:06:24.615 --> 00:06:25.740
So you don't even need to--

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:26.380
MATHIAS MEYER: Yes, exactly.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.770
You want to be notified
when something happens.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.490
The definition of
Continuous Integration

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:33.590
actually goes a lot
further than that.

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:39.030
Depending on who you ask, it
is also a development practice.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:42.010
It's a practice of
continuously integrating

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:44.453
your code into trunk.

00:06:44.453 --> 00:06:48.350
So it's a very
controversial thing, still,

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:50.830
to make sure that no
changes that you make

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:55.849
will live off the main
trunk for longer than a day.

00:06:55.849 --> 00:06:57.390
Extreme programming
had this practice

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.181
where, at the end of
the day, if you're not

00:06:59.181 --> 00:07:01.227
done with your change,
you delete the code.

00:07:01.227 --> 00:07:02.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.480
MARK MANDEL: Wow. [CHUCKLE]

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.597
MATHIAS MEYER: It's very
controversial, still,

00:07:05.597 --> 00:07:06.180
at this point.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.290
It was like, we're
now-- I don't know

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:10.440
how many years
post-extreme programming--

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:11.940
15, 20 years, almost.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:12.050
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:12.420
Something like that.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:12.890
Yeah.

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:13.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:14.938
I was thinking extreme
programming-- that

00:07:14.938 --> 00:07:17.600
is what Windows XP stands for?

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.350
MATHIAS MEYER: No.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:19.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Was it? [LAUGHTER]

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:20.100
MATHIAS MEYER: It was, it was.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:20.600
Yeah.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:23.580
It was XP for short, but I'm
not sure they have [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:24.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah. [LAUGHTER]

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:25.710
MATHIAS MEYER: I think it goes
back a lot further than that.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:28.380
No, it was a set of practices
like good development

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:28.920
practices.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:32.590
One of them that we also
tried to live in the company

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.680
is to go home tired
but not exhausted-- you

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.180
know, like, to find a
good balance in coding.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:41.430
You know, you don't want
to spend 12 hours a day

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:42.210
working on code.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:44.720
You want to give
it your best, say,

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.470
at a normal pace
for 8 hours a day.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:50.700
At the end of the day, your
code is hopefully checked in,

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:53.840
and you just go home
and have a life outside

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.695
of work, which is definitely
one of my favorite extreme

00:07:56.695 --> 00:07:57.622
programming practices.

00:07:57.622 --> 00:07:58.580
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:00.350
So we talked about
Continuous Integration.

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:04.212
How does Travis CI enable you
to do Continuous Integration?

00:08:04.212 --> 00:08:05.670
MATHIAS MEYER: So
one of the things

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:09.880
we pride ourselves in is our
easy integration with GitHub.

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:11.804
As I mentioned
before, we have-- I

00:08:11.804 --> 00:08:13.970
don't know how many hundred
thousands of open source

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:15.970
projects-- somewhere
around 350,000

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:17.730
at this point
running on Travis CI.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:21.390
And it's just really
easy to set up.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:23.940
You sign up with
your GitHub account.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:26.350
We synchronize all of your
repositories from GitHub,

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:30.890
and you just activate one,
add a file to your code repo,

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:33.620
which is a build definition.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:34.970
You can customize build steps.

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:39.289
We have a couple of sensible
defaults for common languages.

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:41.280
But you can customize
a build to your liking,

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.350
so as soon as you enable it, you
can just start checking code,

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:45.680
and we'll start building it.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:47.780
So it's just really,
really easy to get started.

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:49.630
Usually, it just takes minutes.

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.570
MARK MANDEL: So basically, I can
sit down, take my open source

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:54.111
code, and I don't need to worry
about building infrastructure

00:08:54.111 --> 00:08:55.760
around doing my
Continuous Integration.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:57.680
I can just work on my
project, check it in.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.500
And as soon as I check it
in, it runs all my tests

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.720
through the-- I guess it's
a Travis YAML or something?

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.970
MATHIAS MEYER: Yes, the file
is called dot Travis YAML,

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:05.303
and it's part of your code repo.

00:09:05.303 --> 00:09:09.469
So we give developers the power
to have full control over their

00:09:09.469 --> 00:09:11.760
build, which is definitely
something people appreciate.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:16.010
And also, the build definition
itself is part of the code.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:16.510
You know?

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:17.830
It's [? version ?]
in the same way,

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:19.770
and you can trace any
changes that you made.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:22.620
You can trace the
entire history.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:23.370
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:25.280
And then, as I'm building,
it will notify me

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:26.129
if something breaks?

00:09:26.129 --> 00:09:26.920
MATHIAS MEYER: Yes.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:29.180
MARK MANDEL: I assume then also,
if I get, like, a pull request

00:09:29.180 --> 00:09:30.390
or something like
that for my code,

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:31.840
does that also run so I
can see whether it should

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:34.440
be merged in case something bad
happened or anything like that?

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:35.273
MATHIAS MEYER: Yeah.

00:09:35.273 --> 00:09:37.000
That's definitely
one of the reasons

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.240
why Travis CI exists,
especially in open source.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:42.230
There was a time
when you got a pull

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:43.660
request to your repository.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.740
I think early on,
in GitHub terms,

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.390
it was a [? 4q ?]
or before that,

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.252
you'd just get a
patch via e-mail.

00:09:50.252 --> 00:09:51.710
MARK MANDEL: I
remember those days.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.880
MATHIAS MEYER: Old,
old, back in the day.

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.800
And you would merge
it, and then you

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:59.010
committed to your trunk branch.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:02.292
You would hopefully have a CI
running, and only then would

00:10:02.292 --> 00:10:03.750
you know if it's
actually working--

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:05.180
if that patch is working.

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:08.220
So you would either be happy
because a patch is working,

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.732
or you would scream in
agony because now, you

00:10:10.732 --> 00:10:12.440
have this patch to
maintain and make sure

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:15.790
that you fix a build
that someone else broke.

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:18.300
And that was one of
the core problems

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:20.110
that Travis tried to solve.

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:22.442
We now have a native
integration with pull requests

00:10:22.442 --> 00:10:24.150
that's, like, if
there's a project that's

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:27.120
running and using Travis CI,
and someone sends a pull request

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:31.820
to it, we take the
merge between the branch

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:36.860
it's sent to and the downstream
changes, and test that part.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:39.447
So we focus, really,
on the integration

00:10:39.447 --> 00:10:40.280
between all of them.

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:42.800
So it's like a different kind
of Continuous Integration,

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:47.420
if you will-- and make sure
that the change works together

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:49.814
with wherever the trunk
branch is at the moment.

00:10:49.814 --> 00:10:50.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:55.067
And I think that that is free,
if you're writing open source?

00:10:55.067 --> 00:10:55.900
MATHIAS MEYER: Yeah.

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:57.810
If you're running a
open source project,

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:59.704
the usage is completely
free on Travis CI.

00:10:59.704 --> 00:11:00.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:02.409
What if you're not
running open source?

00:11:02.409 --> 00:11:04.700
What if you have a private
repo or something like that?

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:06.491
MATHIAS MEYER: If you
have a private repos,

00:11:06.491 --> 00:11:09.154
we have a hosted platform for
that, which is a paid platform.

00:11:09.154 --> 00:11:10.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:13.067
But you still support it even
if the code is not open source?

00:11:13.067 --> 00:11:13.900
MATHIAS MEYER: Yeah.

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:14.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:15.190
MARK MANDEL: Very cool.

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:16.010
No, that sounds really good.

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.450
It means also, like, if you're
following that internal pull

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:20.741
request model or you want to
do code reviews internally

00:11:20.741 --> 00:11:23.100
when you're building stuff,
then it's all the same.

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:24.600
MATHIAS MEYER: It's a
fairly common model now,

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:26.990
even in companies, to work
like-- you know, GitHub flows.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:29.250
Basically, a good
name for it-- GitHub

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:30.690
has a good
documentation of this.

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:32.770
You start working
on a future branch,

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:34.320
you start opening
a pull request,

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:35.820
you iterate around
the pull request,

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:40.150
and as soon as a pull
request is updated,

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:41.880
we automatically
rerun the build.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:46.010
GitHub tracks the merge
between the downstream branch

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:49.244
and the upstream branch for us,
so we can just take that commit

00:11:49.244 --> 00:11:50.660
and test it all
the time, whenever

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:51.950
the pull request is updated.

00:11:51.950 --> 00:11:54.420
So once you actually
want to merge it,

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:56.410
you're at the point
where you can be certain

00:11:56.410 --> 00:12:00.160
that it's going to work
against the master branch

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:02.369
or whatever branch you open
the pull request against.

00:12:02.369 --> 00:12:03.284
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:12:03.284 --> 00:12:04.980
And I know that
nowadays, I'm not

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:07.210
able to work with
Travis CI or something

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:09.570
similar to that, because
it is very painful when

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:10.570
you change to something.

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:12.420
It was, like, oh,
yeah, I totally

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:13.490
did not break the build.

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:14.910
And, it was, like, oh, I did.

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:18.160
So yeah, it is very good to see
it before you do anything else,

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:20.799
especially pull requests from
people that you might trust.

00:12:20.799 --> 00:12:22.340
And all of a sudden,
it was like, oh,

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:23.740
actually that is wrong.

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:25.677
Also, we've been
using it internally

00:12:25.677 --> 00:12:27.260
in one of the projects
I'm working on,

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:30.720
where we not only run
tests, but also we

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:34.560
run things like styling, spell
checks, and stuff like that.

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:36.690
Like, yeah, you just
misspelled something

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:38.466
on a comment that
will break the build.

00:12:38.466 --> 00:12:39.590
MATHIAS MEYER: Interesting.

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:41.740
People have built interesting
things about this.

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:44.690
Like, there's a conference
that's currently planning it--

00:12:44.690 --> 00:12:47.509
I just saw this
yesterday-- who have

00:12:47.509 --> 00:12:49.800
a site that they're running
on Travis, the static page.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.710
But part of that page
is a JavaScript file,

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:55.720
which runs on Node.js and
it calculates their budget.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:58.170
So it's going to fail the build
if the budget is negative.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:12:59.253
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, nice.

00:12:59.253 --> 00:13:01.880
MATHIAS MEYER: And you
know, if the budget is still

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:05.030
positive in the green, it's
just going to succeed the build.

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:07.694
So people have built interesting
things around Travis.

00:13:07.694 --> 00:13:08.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:09.720
That is very cool.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:11.160
MATHIAS MEYER: So whenever
you send a pull request,

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.409
it says, well, we need to
make this budget adjustment.

00:13:13.409 --> 00:13:14.630
And I was, like, no, no.

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:17.040
We don't have enough-- no
money for that anymore.

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:18.623
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
You're missing money.

00:13:18.623 --> 00:13:19.180
Nice.

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:23.490
So talking about microservices
and stuff like that,

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.735
very often, Continuous
Integration comes up.

00:13:25.735 --> 00:13:28.110
But also, there's another
thing that comes up very often,

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:30.630
which is Continuous Delivery.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:32.760
What is the relationship
between both?

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:35.490
MATHIAS MEYER: So it depends
on who you ask, I would say.

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:38.630
Continuous Delivery
is usually associated

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:42.990
with complex pipelines, like
your build runs through stages,

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:46.860
you make a commit, you start,
say, running unit tests,

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:51.239
and then you have maybe a QA
stage that is manually driven,

00:13:51.239 --> 00:13:52.780
and then you have
a deployment stage,

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:54.920
which eventually gets your
code either to staging

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:55.760
or to production.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:00.152
And for me, the difference
between CI and CD--

00:14:00.152 --> 00:14:02.610
so Continuous Integration and
Continuous Delivery-- really,

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:05.020
really is rather simple.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:08.400
CI basically means
you test every commit,

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:11.920
you make sure that any change
that you make still works,

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:14.870
or it could be pushed
into production.

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:19.320
And the Continuous Delivery--
beyond all these pipelines

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:23.820
and the idea of shipping,
Continuous Delivery

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.560
is, to me, more simply
the idea that you

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:29.190
could ship your code any time.

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:30.820
Like, the code that
you check in could

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:32.670
be deployed at any moment.

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:36.610
So you adjust your mental model
of how you work around the code

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:37.310
accordingly.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:38.060
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:41.017
So even if you've just, like,
pushed it to staging for QA--

00:14:41.017 --> 00:14:43.100
but then, I mean, that's
basically the same thing,

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:43.290
right?

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:44.585
As long as you've got
a push-button solution

00:14:44.585 --> 00:14:46.335
or something like that,
you're just, like,

00:14:46.335 --> 00:14:47.310
that's good to go now.

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:48.226
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:14:48.226 --> 00:14:50.950
So I'd like to talk now a
little bit about Travis itself

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:52.130
and how Travis works.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:54.550
So the first question
is, does Travis

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:56.740
use Continuous
Integration internally?

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:58.430
MATHIAS MEYER: (CHUCKLING) Yes.

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:00.499
Travis CI uses Travis
CI for a lot of things.

00:15:00.499 --> 00:15:02.040
MARK MANDEL: Yay,
inception. [LAUGHS]

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:03.360
MATHIAS MEYER: Yeah.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.420
I mean, that's your
first litmus test

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.620
for any kind of
infrastructure solution.

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:09.830
Can it maintain itself?

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:12.220
And it's been-- I
mean, it was originally

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:14.950
built as a single, not
multi-tenant, solution.

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:18.020
It was originally
built as something one

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:20.640
of our co-founders
wanted to run on-- he

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.340
didn't want to run Jenkins.

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:24.589
He wanted to have something
that runs in Heroku.

00:15:24.589 --> 00:15:26.880
And so he started adding a
couple of nice features like

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:29.190
live build [? log ?]
streaming and other stuff.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:32.350
And based around that,
even the early Travis code

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:34.920
was-- even six years ago,
the early Travis code

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:36.190
was running on itself.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:36.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:37.961
MATHIAS MEYER: And it still is.

00:15:37.961 --> 00:15:39.810
Like, we're avid
users, and we also

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:42.260
have a project which is
a canary build system,

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:44.550
which continuously runs
build on Travis to check

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:45.690
the entire system.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:46.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice. [CHUCKLE]

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:48.460
MATHIAS MEYER: This is a fairly
active project at this point.

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:49.376
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:15:49.376 --> 00:15:50.000
Cool.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:53.770
And what about
Continuous Delivery?

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:57.580
MATHIAS MEYER: We deploy-- our
general approach is that people

00:15:57.580 --> 00:15:59.650
work on feature branches.

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:01.500
We try to make them short-lived.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:06.280
Some live longer, but the
idea's still that, you know,

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:09.150
trunk or [INAUDIBLE] is
deployed or should be deployable

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:09.930
any time.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:11.890
It's still one of the
goals, and you know,

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:14.265
there's a good chance it's
going to be deployed any time.

00:16:14.265 --> 00:16:16.240
We don't yet count
deploys per day.

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:19.870
It's a fairly popular
metric at this point,

00:16:19.870 --> 00:16:24.270
but we're generally very
flexible about people

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:24.920
deploying.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:27.760
Like, we try to keep
the master branch clean.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:28.569
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:16:28.569 --> 00:16:31.235
So I guess the big question is--
you're here on the Google Cloud

00:16:31.235 --> 00:16:32.180
Platform Podcast.

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:33.719
Do you run on Google
Cloud Platform?

00:16:33.719 --> 00:16:34.510
MATHIAS MEYER: Yes.

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:38.110
A part of our infrastructure
runs on Google Compute Engine,

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:42.120
and that part also uses
Google Cloud Storage--

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:44.341
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:16:44.341 --> 00:16:46.590
MATHIAS MEYER: --specifically
to run it to use caches.

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:50.940
Like, we have the system
where most projects use

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:52.480
a certain set of dependencies.

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:54.130
It's true for any
kind of language.

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:57.850
Any kind of language has
some package management tool,

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:00.500
and the installation,
depending on the language,

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:04.430
can either be a matter
of seconds or minutes.

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:06.069
So we have this
built-in caching,

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:08.230
where you can
specify a directory

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:10.829
or, say, bundle your
integration as native,

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:13.020
or NPM integration
as well, where

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:16.369
we take the entire set of
dependencies and, you know,

00:17:16.369 --> 00:17:20.890
tar it up, simply, and upload
it to Google Cloud Storage,

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:21.569
in this case.

00:17:21.569 --> 00:17:23.440
And so in the next
build, it's going

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:28.410
to be pulled down and
extracted, and, you know,

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:30.725
if it's a reasonably good
package management system,

00:17:30.725 --> 00:17:32.600
it's just going to detect
that there's always

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:33.970
already something there.

00:17:33.970 --> 00:17:36.940
So it's just going to check for
any changes and install those.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.254
And it speeds up
builds significantly.

00:17:39.254 --> 00:17:40.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:42.710
So when you say that you're
running on Google Compute

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:46.160
Engine, does that mean
that for every build task

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:50.044
that you spawn, you spend a
new computed engine instance?

00:17:50.044 --> 00:17:50.960
Or how does that work?

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:52.850
MATHIAS MEYER: Yeah.

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:55.680
For builds that run on
that platform, whenever

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:57.770
we get a commit
notification with GitHub,

00:17:57.770 --> 00:17:59.980
we go through the process
of creating the build

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:01.260
and scheduling it.

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:05.010
And once we start
executing it, every build

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:06.200
runs in a clean environment.

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:09.302
That was one of the early
differentiators of Travis CI--

00:18:09.302 --> 00:18:10.760
that you get a
vanilla environment.

00:18:10.760 --> 00:18:14.499
It's going to be restarted
from scratch for every build,

00:18:14.499 --> 00:18:15.540
and you can rely on that.

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:18.040
So on Google Compute
Engine, we start up

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:21.140
a new instance for every build.

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:23.690
And one of the things that
we found there was, like, we

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:27.970
get fairly stable boot times
on Google Compute Engine, which

00:18:27.970 --> 00:18:29.380
makes it a lot more reliable.

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:31.754
And that was one reason
why we chose it in the end.

00:18:31.754 --> 00:18:32.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:33.170
Yes.

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:36.060
So have you ever considered
doing some things differently?

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:40.300
Like, instead of running one
new VM per [INAUDIBLE], running

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:42.050
one new container, or
something like that?

00:18:42.050 --> 00:18:42.850
MATHIAS MEYER: Yes.

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:44.266
Another part of
our infrastructure

00:18:44.266 --> 00:18:45.193
does exactly that.

00:18:45.193 --> 00:18:46.276
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, cool.

00:18:46.276 --> 00:18:47.650
MATHIAS MEYER:
That part actually

00:18:47.650 --> 00:18:50.760
use Docker to create
the build environment.

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:54.110
And that's just super fast.

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:56.810
Like, I just looked up
the stats for how long

00:18:56.810 --> 00:18:59.210
it takes to boot up a
dock container for us.

00:18:59.210 --> 00:19:01.670
And I think the 95
percentile of that

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:05.920
is 3.3 seconds for-- so
this is-- when you look

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:07.880
at Travis while you're
committing something,

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:10.150
you, pretty much, see the
build start instantly.

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:10.900
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:12.934
MATHIAS MEYER: If it
goes through the system

00:19:12.934 --> 00:19:14.600
within a second, or
something like that,

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:17.050
the Docker container
is just booted up,

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:19.520
and it starts running
almost instantly.

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:22.040
And that part-- we're still
playing around with that.

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:25.612
There is still something missing
on Google Compute Engine that

00:19:25.612 --> 00:19:27.320
keeps us from running
that part on there,

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:29.120
but technically, it
could run anywhere.

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:29.900
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:30.870
That's pretty cool.

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:33.559
So I was curious about
how you're controlling

00:19:33.559 --> 00:19:34.850
those Compute Engine instances.

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:37.480
I know we have a series of
APIs, but, like, what does

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:40.470
the lifecycle that look like?

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:46.220
MATHIAS MEYER: So we
hit the API pretty hard.

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:48.320
I'm sorry about that.

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:51.840
So for every build, we send
an API request to create

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:54.950
an instance, and we keep
pulling the API for the status

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:56.550
of that virtual server.

00:19:56.550 --> 00:20:00.110
And once it's ready,
the entire build

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:02.160
is executed through
an SSH connection.

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:04.480
So once it's ready, we SSH in.

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:08.570
We compile the build script,
which is uploaded and run

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:09.640
on the virtual server.

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:10.390
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:11.931
MATHIAS MEYER: That's
pretty much it.

00:20:11.931 --> 00:20:14.486
And at the end of the
tests, it's just terminated.

00:20:14.486 --> 00:20:15.610
MARK MANDEL: And it's done.

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:15.940
MATHIAS MEYER: Yeah.

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:17.360
MARK MANDEL: We're finished.

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:18.430
Sounds good.

00:20:18.430 --> 00:20:20.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
was wondering, also,

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:23.505
regarding containers--
since you're

00:20:23.505 --> 00:20:25.880
starting a new one every single
time, have you considered

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:28.430
doing things like Kubernetes or
Container Engine or something

00:20:28.430 --> 00:20:28.930
like that?

00:20:28.930 --> 00:20:30.477
What do you think about those?

00:20:30.477 --> 00:20:32.060
MATHIAS MEYER: We've
thought about it.

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:34.110
We just haven't
gotten around to this.

00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:34.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:20:34.410 --> 00:20:35.300
MATHIAS MEYER: It's
definitely something

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:37.216
we have in the back of
our mind to play around

00:20:37.216 --> 00:20:39.057
with that a little bit.

00:20:39.057 --> 00:20:40.640
Basically, we have
our own scheduling,

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:44.360
and maybe there are
ways to optimize that.

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:46.222
But we haven't
gotten around to it.

00:20:46.222 --> 00:20:48.180
It's definitely something
we have on the radar.

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:49.096
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:49.096 --> 00:20:51.090
Is that scheduling open
source, by any chance?

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:53.090
MATHIAS MEYER: Most of
Travis CI is open source,

00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:55.360
and I think that
part in particular

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:57.480
is-- yes, everything
about that is open source.

00:20:57.480 --> 00:20:59.180
Like, the entire
build execution part

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:03.440
and our scheduler, which
is really, really simple,

00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:06.352
sounds way fancier
than it really is.

00:21:06.352 --> 00:21:10.210
But it is a scheduler, and
all of these components

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:11.130
are open source.

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:12.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very good.

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:15.460
We'll put a link to the
repo in the show notes.

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:17.720
There's one more question
that I wanted to ask.

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:20.730
I've been curious about
it for quite a long time.

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:24.573
I assume that there could be a
Travis somewhere in Travis CI.

00:21:24.573 --> 00:21:27.600
Why is Travis CI called, Travis?

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.220
MATHIAS MEYER: So that
story goes back to-- well,

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:33.922
first of all, people tend to
think that someone in our team

00:21:33.922 --> 00:21:34.630
is named, Travis.

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:36.455
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, I was expecting.

00:21:36.455 --> 00:21:38.830
MATHIAS MEYER: I was called
Travis on multiple occasions,

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:42.409
but it goes back
to Bob the Builder.

00:21:42.409 --> 00:21:43.075
MARK MANDEL: Oh.

00:21:43.075 --> 00:21:43.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, wow.

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:44.813
MATHIAS MEYER: Yeah.

00:21:44.813 --> 00:21:47.110
That's the response that
usually comes back from that.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:50.305
Bob has a tractor, and that
tractor's name is Travis.

00:21:50.305 --> 00:21:51.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Wow.

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:51.679
OK.

00:21:51.679 --> 00:21:53.436
I really did not
expect that answer.

00:21:53.436 --> 00:21:54.061
That's awesome.

00:21:54.061 --> 00:21:56.490
MARK MANDEL: We need a picture
of that tractor on the show

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:56.990
notes.

00:21:56.990 --> 00:22:01.660
MATHIAS MEYER: The story around
that is, in the early stages,

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:03.750
Travis used a tool
which was called,

00:22:03.750 --> 00:22:07.440
Bob, which was just a
simple Ruby script that

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:09.820
was used to run builds.

00:22:09.820 --> 00:22:12.670
And so it was Bob and Travis,
just working together.

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:13.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There you go.

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:14.832
This is Travis.

00:22:14.832 --> 00:22:15.790
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:22:15.790 --> 00:22:17.510
No one else can see that,
because we're on a podcast.

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:19.090
But thanks for
showing it. [CHUCKLE]

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:21.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We'll
put a picture of this.

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:21.530
OK.

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:22.450
I did not know.

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:25.629
I knew, somehow, Bob the
Builder, but not Travis.

00:22:25.629 --> 00:22:27.920
MARK MANDEL: I also have to
ask because I can't believe

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:28.870
Francesc hasn't.

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:30.890
What languages do
you use at Travis CI?

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:32.170
MATHIAS MEYER: OK.

00:22:32.170 --> 00:22:36.420
So we used to be a
very Ruby-heavy shop,

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:41.900
and a lot of us come from the
Ruby open source community.

00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:47.330
So the earliest versions of
Travis were built in Ruby.

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:49.870
And at some point,
for a while, they

00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:51.362
ran on JRuby in production.

00:22:51.362 --> 00:22:52.070
MARK MANDEL: Ooh.

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:54.147
I have a really
soft spot for JRuby.

00:22:54.147 --> 00:22:54.980
MATHIAS MEYER: Yeah.

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:59.030
It's allowed us to scale
up in interesting ways.

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:01.160
Nowadays, most of our
components are just

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:04.030
running on C, Ruby,
and MRI, because it's

00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:05.820
less resource-intensive.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:09.070
And the build execution
part-- so the part

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:10.930
that, you know,
creates instances

00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:14.120
and runs the builds-- these
are now mostly written in Go.

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:15.770
We have parts of
our infrastructure

00:23:15.770 --> 00:23:18.190
that are still running
on an old JRuby system,

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:21.362
but we're going to replace
that with the Go execution.

00:23:21.362 --> 00:23:22.320
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:22.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very cool.

00:23:22.970 --> 00:23:25.261
MARK MANDEL: What made you
decide to write stuff in Go?

00:23:25.261 --> 00:23:26.928
How did you end up
with that conclusion?

00:23:26.928 --> 00:23:29.219
MATHIAS MEYER: That's a
question I can't really answer,

00:23:29.219 --> 00:23:29.950
I'm afraid.

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:30.560
MARK MANDEL: Oh. [LAUGHS]

00:23:30.560 --> 00:23:31.900
MATHIAS MEYER: It just happened.

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.899
That was the team decision,
so I'm [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:33.899 --> 00:23:35.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's just
because Go is amazing.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:36.490
Probably.

00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:37.850
MATHIAS MEYER: Go is amazing.

00:23:37.850 --> 00:23:39.490
MARK MANDEL: If people
want to try Travis CI out,

00:23:39.490 --> 00:23:40.280
